[{:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn", :item-id "https://tis-100.wincon.dev/", :title "LLMs Play Zachtronics' TIS-100", :link "https://tis-100.wincon.dev/", :published-at #inst "2026-11-11T00:37:05.000-00:00", :content "Comments"}, :excerpts [{:text "LLMs Play Zachtronics' TIS-100", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :title}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :title "vim-slime", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :published-at #inst "2026-11-11T04:42:09.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> vim-slime </h1> <p> Mar 26, 2012 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>Today I found the awesomeness that is <a href=\"https://github.com/jpalardy/vim-slime\">vim-slime</a>, it's been an exciting day for me. <a href=\"http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/\">Slime</a> is the \"The Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for Emacs\", I can almost hear the emacs crowd laughing.</p> <p>For those that use vim and haven't used Slime, vim-slime or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's awesome:</p> <p><strong>Text can be sent from any process to the stdin of a <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/software/screen/\">gnu screen</a> or <a href=\"http://tmux.sourceforge.net/\">tmux</a> session. The process in this case is vim and the screen/tmux session is a terminal</strong>.</p> <p>Screen is a <a href=\"/journal/oh-screen-where-have-you-been\">really neat</a> terminal multiplexer (you can run multiple terminals in a terminal window). The multiplexed shell processes are children of the screen process, which itself is not a child of the terminal window process. This means a screen process and its child processes keep running if you close the terminal window. Later you can re-connect to it, this is what makes vim-slime possible.</p> <p>Here is an screen shot, on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim slime screenshot\" src=\"screenshot.jpg\"/></p> <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs that use STDIN.</p> <p>This concept can be taken even further, You can even connect to a tmux session over SSH and share a terminal or a <a href=\"http://remotepairprogramming.com/remote-pair-programming-with-tmux-and-vim-the\">terminal program like vim to do remote pairing</a>!</p> <p>Hopefully remote pairing is the topic of my next post as there are a couple geographically distant people I know who are keen to do some pair hacking. I stand to learn a lot!</p> <p><a name=\"footnote-1\">[1]</a> I learnt almost everything I know about Lisp from <a href=\"http://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/matthias/BTLS/\">The Little Schemer</a>. Great book.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl"], :source :content} {:text "... <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl" "command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :title "Swipe Keyboard", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :published-at #inst "2026-11-11T04:42:09.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <style> #swipe-canvas { position: relative; width: 900px; height: 300px; } #swipe-results { font-size: 30px; padding-left: 50px; padding-left: 50px; } #swipe-results ul { margin: 0px; padding: 0px; } #swipe-results li { float: left; background-color: #DDDDDD; list-style-type: none; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; border-radius: 5px; } #swipe { position: relative; } #swipe-loading { position: absolute; height: 50px; width: 300px; top: 85px; left: 300px; background-color: darkgray; border-radius: 10px; text-align: center; padding-top: 20px; border: black; border-width: 5px; } </style> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> Swipe Keyboard </h1> <p> Apr 06, 2014 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>When I first tried a <a href=\"http://www.swype.com/\">Swype</a> keyboard I was impressed how effective it was. Even though I don't use the feature on my phone I was interested in how it could be built, so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard\">implemented this otherwise useless swipe-able keyboard</a> below. It probably doesn't work on mobile devices, but works on modern browsers with mouse pointers (although I've only really tried Chrome and Firefox).</p> <div id=\"swipe\"> <canvas height=\"300px\" id=\"swipe-canvas\" width=\"900px\"></canvas> <div id=\"swipe-results\"></div> <div style=\"clear: both\"></div> <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially tried to solve this using the technique Peter Norvig famously uses in his <a href=\"http://norvig.com/spell-correct.html]\">spell checker</a>. He takes a sequence of characters and generates a set of word candidates by adding, removing and swapping characters in the original sequence, the generated candidates are removed if they are not found a dictionary. This can work but to be effective too many combinations need to be generated.</p> <p>If the dictionary is indexed into a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie\">trie</a> the number of combinations generated can be reduced significantly by traversing the trie and only generating valid letter combinations. This is a pretty bare implementation of that, it requires: </p> <ul> <li>The first and last characters of the initial sequence are used </li> <li>Intermediate characters in the initial sequence can be repeated or discarded </li> <li>No characters are added or swapped</li> </ul> <p>Basically, if you swipe through all the characters in a word in order, then the word will be found if it is in the index regardless how many characters are swiped in between. It is surprisingly quick and effective.</p> <p>This implementation uses <a href=\"https://raw.github.com/first20hours/google-10000-english/master/google-10000-english.txt\">these 10000 words</a>, I intended to use digital books but never got around to it as these words demonstrate the concept well enough.</p> <p>This is the first thing I've written in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from non-idiomatic to shamblolic. I initially used a <a href=\"http://clojuredocs.org/clojure_core/clojure.zip/zipper\">zipper</a> to build the trie with immutable data structures, but found the indexing took to long with my zipper implementation so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard/commit/6edd7b26e78121fbe8586b3f0ef54ca8277d9e32\">switched to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/core.async\">core.async</a> library is really awesome, the <a href=\"http://docs.closure-library.googlecode.com/git/index.html\">Google closure library</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler/\">compiler</a> integration with <a href=\"http://leiningen.org/\">Leiningen</a> the <a href=\"https://github.com/emezeske/lein-cljsbuild\">cljsbuild plug-in</a> to be impressive. My main pains were the slow JVM start-up time, the advanced closure compiler build of the web worker script fails silently when run (but the main script works fine when compiled with the advanced compiler), and at times I felt some compile time type checking would be nice.</p> <p>I would like to extend this experiment to index the word occurrence counts and proceeding word counts in original text and rank the found words as most likely. Support casing, umlauts, special characters, spelling correction and compound words in the indexing and lookup. I think a live lookup while swiping would also be possible.</p> <p>Overall this was fun, turned out OK I think, and was a great learning experience.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> <script src=\"swipe.js\" type=\"text/javascript\"></script> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "sean-goedecke", :item-id "https://seangoedecke.com/glue-work-is-still-harmful-in-the-age-of-ai/", :title "Glue work is still harmful in the age of AI", :link "https://seangoedecke.com/glue-work-is-still-harmful-in-the-age-of-ai/", :published-at #inst "2026-11-11T00:00:00.000-00:00", :content "I am not a believer in glue work1. Processes that can only be sustained by the unrewarded sacrifice of engineers ought to die. Now that LLMs are writing most of our code, does that free human engineers up to do more glue work? No, it doesn’t. I think glue work is actually less important to companies than it was in the 2010s. Let’s go through four reasons why. First, AI decreases the amount of coordination needed to ship. The magnifying effect of AI agents means that any single engineer can now take on projects that would previously have needed a small team. A lot of glue work was helping with coordination: booking kickoff and status meetings, passing around internal updates, and so on. A single engineer doesn’t need to do any of that. Second, engineers in the 2020s require less “careful handling”. In my experience, the most important subcategory of coordination-based glue work was managing difficult people. Many companies have secret gatekeepers: engineers with outsized influence and large egos who will shoot down your project if they feel insufficiently consulted. I’ve written before about how some teams are structurally set up to be gatekeepers, and how some engineers could get by in the 2010s by basically saying no to everything. The end of ZIRP changed all that. Many of the prickliest gatekeepers have been forced out of the industry or coerced into changing their attitude, which reduces the amount of necessary glue work required to ship. Third, people are hiring fewer juniors. This is probably a bad thing for the industry, but it does also eliminate a big category of glue work. Experienced hires need less hand-holding, so companies don’t need people to quietly volunteer to onboard new hires. Fourth, write-only reports can be automated with LLMs. A lot of glue work was what I call “proof of work”: writing a report to demonstrate that you’re aware of what your team is doing, or filling in a template to satisfy some legal requirement. This was often work that nobody wanted to do, so it relied on someone volunteering to “be glue”. Now reports like these can be quickly turned out with LLMs. It doesn’t really matter that nobody wants to read LLM text, because the point of these reports was never to be read anyway. Can LLMs just automate all glue work? Absolutely not. Many engineers are trying to do this out of a kind of manic optimism, but it’s a really bad idea. Write-only reports are one thing, but communicating to other engineers cannot be delegated to an LLM. When you need to check in on the status of a project, or respond to someone else’s ping, or anything like that, you should compose and write the message yourself. Nothing will torch your relationship with other engineers as quickly as making them talk to your Claude Opus instance. So where does that leave us? More or less where we were in 2024, I think. Avoid at all costs becoming “the glue work person”, let bad processes fail so they can be changed, but be willing to do small amounts of tactical glue work where it can meaningfully contribute to the success of your projects. Most of your time and effort should still be spent on the code: on driving AI agents and aligning them with your company’s values. Glue work is all the squishy “non-technical” work that goes into building software: making sure teams are talking to each other, writing reports, updating docs, onboarding engineers, and so on. Specifically it’s the parts of that work that are not included in the formal process, and that only really get done if someone volunteers to do it. For more on why I don’t like it, see my 2024 post Glue work considered harmful. ↩"}, :excerpts [{:text "... sacrifice of engineers ought to die. Now that LLMs are writing most of our code, does that free...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :content} {:text "... Fourth, write-only reports can be automated with LLMs. A lot of glue work was what I call “proof of...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :content} {:text "... like these can be quickly turned out with LLMs. It doesn’t really matter that nobody wants to read LLM text, because the point of these reports was never to be read anyway. Can LLMs just automate all glue work? Absolutely not....", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :content}]}]